#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096464 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9688df89-056e-59d7-869e-9841715b2aea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7a0aba38-1167-5365-bbd2-cd2ba7e57b15" :question/math-academy-id "e-612" :question/problem "Find $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\langle 6t^5+2t, \\sec^2 t\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Note that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle 6t^5+2t,\\, \\sec^2 t\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫⟨6t^{5} + 2t, \\sec^{2} t⟩dt &= ⟨∫(6t^{5} + 2t)dt, ∫\\sec^{2} tdt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{6} + t^{2} + c_{1}, \\tan t + c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{6} + t^{2}, \\tan t⟩ + ⟨c_{1}, c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨t^{6} + t^{2}, \\tan t⟩ + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf C$ is an arbitrary (vector) constant of integration."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096465 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53fca396-4ade-5180-9c9b-a73f24f5b5b8" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/id #uuid "7a39ddf2-3ba2-534f-8dca-93497cae823f" :question/math-academy-id "e-613" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\left\\langle\\cos(2t+1), \\, 4\\right\\rangle \\,\\textrm d t\\,$." :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n∫⟨\\cos (2t + 1), 4⟩dt = ⟨∫\\cos (2t + 1)dt, ∫4dt⟩\n$$\n\n- For $x(t) =\\cos(2t+1)$, we calculate its integral using substitution. So, we let $u= 2t +1$. Then\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d t} = 2\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\textrm d t = \\dfrac 1 2 \\textrm d u$.\nTherefore, we have\n$∫x(t)dt|= ∫\\cos (2t + 1)dt; = ∫\\cos u \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}du; = \\frac{1}{2}∫\\cos udu; = \\frac{1}{2}(\\sin u) + c_{1}; = \\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1) + c_{1}$.\n- For $y(t) =4$, we have\n$∫y(t)dt = ∫4dt = 4t + c_{2}$.\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫⟨\\cos (2t + 1), 4⟩dt &= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1) + c_{1}, 4t + c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1), 4t⟩ + ⟨c_{1}, c_{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\frac{1}{2}\\sin (2t + 1), 4t⟩ + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096466 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3081ffe7-c448-5e0d-8dd1-808d23199a7f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/id #uuid "6da4ce70-1718-53bb-8831-a93fdc3e4677" :question/math-academy-id "e-614" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{2} \\left\\langle 2e^{t},\\, 6t^2\\right\\rangle\\, \\textrm d t$." :question/worked-solution "To integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{2}⟨2e^{t}, 6t^{2}⟩dt &= ⟨∫_{0}^{2}2e^{t}dt, ∫_{0}^{2}6t^{2}dt⟩. \\\\\n&= ⟨2e^{t} \\mid _{0}^{2}, 2t^{3} \\mid _{0}^{2}⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨[2e^{2} - 2e^{0}], [2 \\cdot 2^{3} - 0]⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨2e^{2} - 2, 16⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096467 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8dd18a5b-d7a1-5590-ae70-f47ff5b464b1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function That Satisfies a Given Initial Condition"}] :question/id #uuid "76a0fefe-71fd-5500-a4d8-7d5f8a09613e" :question/math-academy-id "e-615" :question/problem "Calculate $\\mathbf F(t)$ given that $\\mathbf F'(t)= \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t, \\, \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf F(0)=\\langle 0,\\, 0\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Note that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\int \\mathbf F'(t)\\, \\textrm d t=\\int \\left\\langle \\sec t \\tan t,\\, \\sin(2t)\\right\\rangle \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nTo integrate a vector-valued function, we integrate each component separately. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫⟨\\sec t\\tan t, \\sin (2t)⟩dt &= ⟨∫\\sec t\\tan tdt, ∫\\sin (2t)dt⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\sec t + c_{1},-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2t) + c_{2}⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe initial condition\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(0)=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\ngives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(0) = ⟨\\sec 0 + c_{1},-\\frac{1}{2}\\cos (2 \\cdot 0) + c_{2}⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩ \\\\\n⟨1 + c_{1},-\\frac{1}{2} + c_{2}⟩ &= ⟨0, 0⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $c_1=-1$ and\n\n$$\nc_2=\\dfrac 12\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(t)=\\left\\langle \\sec t - 1, \\, -\\dfrac 1 2\\cos(2t)+ \\dfrac12\\right\\rangle\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9688df89-056e-59d7-869e-9841715b2aea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Antiderivative of a Vector-Valued Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80530319-a6b8-55b9-b731-4ad7a4a59618" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- ⟨e^{t} - 4t, \\cos t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095708} {:answer/id #uuid "e72a7913-4e20-5a4f-a654-a1c67970257e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e^{t},-\\cos t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095709} {:answer/id #uuid "46a55a84-cf37-5290-a61f-d84df6936650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e^{t} - 4t,-\\cos t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095710} {:answer/id #uuid "54d4ed94-c606-5048-9de8-699236ff79a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e^{t} - 4t, \\sin t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095711} {:answer/id #uuid "599e1a14-9e35-5198-93b5-4a66b496dbe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e^{t} - 4t, \\cos t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46a55a84-cf37-5290-a61f-d84df6936650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨e^{t} - 4t,-\\cos t⟩ + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095710} :answer-field/id #uuid "48f6bbb6-84b4-5734-8cf4-b84cb1b06c3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #e